1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,600
Today I want to talk about

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:05,200
what to me is a very interesting

3
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:08,760
topic, and that is futures markets.

4
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:12,560
Not very interesting to most people.

5
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:15,160
Most people have no idea what they are.

6
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:21,320
But I think that futures markets, what we're really

7
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:25,240
talking about is, there's different ways of viewing it.

8
00:00:25,240 --> 00:00:31,119
Futures markets for things like agricultural commodities, or

9
00:00:31,119 --> 00:00:39,000
interest rates, or financial securities are markets about

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:40,000
the future.

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,680
They're markets that, in some sense, predict the future.

12
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:47,640
And future matters, right?

13
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:48,480
We live a life.

14
00:00:48,480 --> 00:00:52,480
We have a long horizon.

15
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:54,480
And we have to be careful.

16
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:55,239
We have to be careful.

17
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:57,240
We have to be careful.

18
00:00:57,240 --> 00:00:59,240
I said before, I think,

19
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:01,240
that your planning horizon must be

20
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:03,240
at least a century.

21
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:04,239
Right?

22
00:01:04,239 --> 00:01:06,640
Because you probably live that long anyway with modern

23
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:09,240
medicine, and you care about other people, too.

24
00:01:09,240 --> 00:01:12,240
So, the beauty of futures

25
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:15,240
markets is that we have prices

26
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:16,240
for the future.

27
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:20,240
We talked already about forward markets, and we talked

28
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:22,240
about forward interest rates.

29
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:24,240
And that is a very interesting topic.

30
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:24,480
And I think that's a very interesting topic.

31
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:26,980
And I think that is

32
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:29,480
related to what we're talking about.

33
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:35,480
Forwards and futures are similar concepts.

34
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:41,480
Futures is the more precise concept, or the more developed

35
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:42,480
concept.

36
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:45,480
And I'll explain the

37
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:48,480
difference between forwards and futures.

38
00:01:48,480 --> 00:01:50,980
But just in a nutshell,

39
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:53,480
futures markets are organized markets,

40
00:01:53,480 --> 00:02:00,480
like the stock market, that trade standardized contracts,

41
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:05,480
representing things that will happen at future dates.

42
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:09,479
And because they're standardized, they're worldwide.

43
00:02:09,479 --> 00:02:12,480
Everybody looks at them and uses them.

44
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:15,980
Whereas forward markets are

45
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:19,480
more specialized markets that typically

46
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:21,480
are not as easy to interpret or as clear.

47
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:22,480
And they're not as easy to interpret or as clear.

48
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:27,980
So, futures markets are, in

49
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:32,480
some sense, more fundamental and important.

50
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:34,480
I have a particular interest.

51
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:36,480
I've been interested in futures

52
00:02:36,480 --> 00:02:38,480
markets myself for many years.

53
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:39,980
And wondering why they're not

54
00:02:39,980 --> 00:02:41,480
even bigger and more important.

55
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:44,980
So, in 1993, I wrote

56
00:02:44,980 --> 00:02:48,480
a book called Macro Markets about,

57
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:49,980
let's make our markets bigger

58
00:02:49,980 --> 00:02:51,480
and more important and more pervasive.

59
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:52,480
And I've been trying to do that.

60
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:55,980
Notably, in 2006, I was

61
00:02:55,980 --> 00:02:59,480
working with the Chicago Mercantile Exchange,

62
00:02:59,480 --> 00:03:03,480
which is the biggest futures market in the world.

63
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:06,480
And we created Home Price Futures.

64
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:08,480
And they've been trading now for five years.

65
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:10,980
But I'm not really going to talk

66
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:13,480
about them, because they have so far disappointed.

67
00:03:13,480 --> 00:03:16,480
They're not important, at least not yet.

68
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:19,480
But I'm going to talk about some important futures markets.

69
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:20,480
And I'm going to talk about

70
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:21,480
some of the most important futures markets.

71
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:30,480
So, let me, first, I want to put

72
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:38,480
a, okay, just a couple of definitions up.

73
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:40,980
Futures, that's what we're going to

74
00:03:40,980 --> 00:03:43,480
talk about most in this lecture.

75
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:46,480
And it has a special meaning in finance.

76
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:50,480
And I was contrasting that to four words.

77
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:54,480
And I'm going to talk about the two.

78
00:03:54,480 --> 00:04:06,480
And so, but both of these together are derivatives.

79
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:14,480
And that means that they, the price in these

80
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:18,480
markets derives from a price in some other market.

81
00:04:18,480 --> 00:04:20,480
There's a primary or secondary market.

82
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:25,980
There's a primary or underlying

83
00:04:25,980 --> 00:04:31,480
market, which has its own price.

84
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:34,980
And then there's a derivative market that

85
00:04:34,980 --> 00:04:38,480
has a futures price or a forward price.

86
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:42,480
I guess I'm speaking in kind of abstract terms.

87
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:47,480
So, let me just start.

88
00:04:47,480 --> 00:04:49,480
I haven't really, I want to give you an example.

89
00:04:49,480 --> 00:04:51,480
And we'll see better what I'm talking about.

90
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:53,980
But let me just first

91
00:04:53,980 --> 00:04:56,480
comment just on this word, derivatives.

92
00:04:56,480 --> 00:05:00,480
To people in finance, derivatives are

93
00:05:00,480 --> 00:05:04,480
an exciting development of financial markets.

94
00:05:04,480 --> 00:05:07,980
We start out with a simple market and we

95
00:05:07,980 --> 00:05:11,480
develop derivative markets that add more detail and information

96
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:15,480
than was in the underlying or primary market.

97
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:17,480
That's exciting.

98
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:18,480
I find it exciting.

99
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:20,980
However, I don't know if you,

100
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:23,480
how often you hear this word derivatives.

101
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:25,480
It's become a four-letter word.

102
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:31,480
It's become an ugly word.

103
00:05:31,480 --> 00:05:32,480
Why is that?

104
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:35,980
I think it's because people blame the current

105
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:39,480
financial crisis on derivatives, whether rightly or wrongly.

106
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:46,480
And it's because I think there's a public anger.

107
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:52,980
There's a public anger about derivatives

108
00:05:52,980 --> 00:05:59,480
that is largely due to misunderstanding.

109
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:02,480
I mentioned before that I think

110
00:06:02,480 --> 00:06:05,480
that finance tends to attract sociopaths.

111
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:07,480
I mentioned, I defined that for you.

112
00:06:07,480 --> 00:06:12,480
People who want to manipulate and fool and deceive people.

113
00:06:12,480 --> 00:06:13,980
But I don't think the financial

114
00:06:13,980 --> 00:06:15,480
community is particularly populated by sociopaths.

115
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:19,480
You might think so, reading some accounts.

116
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:21,480
But I think it's not true.

117
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:25,980
And I think it's not true because the financial

118
00:06:25,980 --> 00:06:30,480
community knows about this problem and ejects such people.

119
00:06:30,480 --> 00:06:33,480
They get caught and you can't make

120
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:36,480
a career in finance if you're a sociopath.

121
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:40,480
So, if you think you have this problem, that you have

122
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:44,480
sociopathic tendencies, I would advise you not to go into finance.

123
00:06:44,480 --> 00:06:45,480
Because you will get caught.

124
00:06:45,480 --> 00:06:47,480
Because you will get caught and ejected.

125
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:49,480
So, save yourself the trouble.

126
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:51,480
Don't go into finance.

127
00:06:51,480 --> 00:06:55,480
Pick some field where you can't hurt anybody.

128
00:06:55,480 --> 00:06:58,480
That would be a smart thing to

129
00:06:58,480 --> 00:07:01,480
do, if you have such a problem.

130
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:07,480
So, I think we need to regulate derivatives markets.

131
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:09,480
And that's what I'm talking about.

132
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:12,480
And we need self-regulation of the markets themselves.

133
00:07:12,480 --> 00:07:14,480
But we need government regulation as well.

134
00:07:14,480 --> 00:07:17,480
But there's nothing evil about derivatives.

135
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:20,980
And in fact, derivatives are fundamental to

136
00:07:20,980 --> 00:07:24,480
the way I think a modern economy works.

137
00:07:24,480 --> 00:07:29,480
So, I had you read an article, I put it

138
00:07:29,480 --> 00:07:34,480
on an earlier section of the reading list, by Charles Conant.

139
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:38,480
Who wrote a book in 1904

140
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:42,480
called Wall Street and the Capital.

141
00:07:42,480 --> 00:07:44,980
And I don't know if you,

142
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:47,480
you should have read that by now.

143
00:07:47,480 --> 00:07:52,480
But he starts out by saying, it's just

144
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:57,480
amazing how public opinion thinks that speculation is evil.

145
00:07:57,480 --> 00:08:01,480
And they don't understand that speculation is just business.

146
00:08:01,480 --> 00:08:06,480
I mean, business decisions involve guesses about the future.

147
00:08:06,480 --> 00:08:08,480
And so, when you have well-developed

148
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:10,480
markets, these guesses become market prices.

149
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:15,480
And so, the prices go

150
00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:20,480
into the calculations that everyone makes.

151
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:24,480
And the calculations are just done better.

152
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:26,480
Somehow, there's just a failure

153
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:28,480
for most people to understand it.

154
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:33,480
And I'm hoping in this lecture to try

155
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:38,480
to give some more understanding of these markets.

156
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:39,480
I'm not sure.

157
00:08:40,480 --> 00:08:42,480
But I think they're fascinating.

158
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:44,480
Most people apparently don't.

159
00:08:44,480 --> 00:08:48,480
Because you don't hear them brought up very much.

160
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:55,480
I wanted to just say a little bit more about

161
00:08:55,480 --> 00:09:02,480
hostility toward derivative markets and futures markets and speculation in general.

162
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:05,480
In 1991, that was 20 years ago, I wrote a

163
00:09:05,480 --> 00:09:08,480
joint paper with a, I don't remember whether I mentioned this.

164
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:09,480
Probably not.

165
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:13,980
With a Russian, a young man from Russia that

166
00:09:13,980 --> 00:09:17,480
I met when I was visiting the Soviet Union.

167
00:09:17,480 --> 00:09:20,480
Just before the end of the Soviet Union.

168
00:09:20,480 --> 00:09:23,980
And we were talking about

169
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:27,480
how antagonistic Russians are toward capitalism.

170
00:09:27,480 --> 00:09:30,480
And he said, this is a big problem.

171
00:09:30,480 --> 00:09:33,480
Russia just can't embrace capitalism.

172
00:09:33,480 --> 00:09:34,480
People hate it.

173
00:09:34,480 --> 00:09:38,480
They hate the profit makers and the financiers.

174
00:09:38,480 --> 00:09:40,480
And I said to him, well, you know.

175
00:09:40,480 --> 00:09:42,480
It's the same in the U.S.

176
00:09:42,480 --> 00:09:44,480
How do you know it's any worse in Russia?

177
00:09:44,480 --> 00:09:47,480
And so, we decided to do a questionnaire survey.

178
00:09:47,480 --> 00:09:49,480
And I was going to read one of the questions.

179
00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:53,480
Comparing people in Moscow and people in New York.

180
00:09:53,480 --> 00:09:56,480
To see which one understands capitalism better.

181
00:09:56,480 --> 00:09:57,980
So, one of the questions

182
00:09:57,980 --> 00:09:59,480
that we had was the following.

183
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:03,480
We wrote these questions and translated them into Russian.

184
00:10:03,480 --> 00:10:05,980
And we tried to make them exactly

185
00:10:05,980 --> 00:10:08,480
the same question between Russian and English.

186
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:09,480
All right.

187
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:12,480
Here's the question we asked in both cities.

188
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:15,480
Grain traders in capitalist countries

189
00:10:15,480 --> 00:10:18,480
sometimes hold grain without selling it.

190
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:21,480
Putting it in temporary storage

191
00:10:21,480 --> 00:10:24,480
in anticipation of higher prices later.

192
00:10:24,480 --> 00:10:28,480
Do you think this speculation will cause more

193
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:32,480
frequent shortages of flour, bread and other grain products?

194
00:10:32,480 --> 00:10:36,480
Or will it cause such shortages to become rarer?

195
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:38,480
What do you think people said?

196
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:41,480
Does the process of speculating in

197
00:10:41,480 --> 00:10:44,480
grain cause problems or solve problems?

198
00:10:44,480 --> 00:10:49,480
Well, we found that in the U.S.A.

199
00:10:49,480 --> 00:10:54,480
66% said it will make shortages more common.

200
00:10:54,480 --> 00:10:58,480
But in the Soviet Union, in Moscow, only

201
00:10:58,480 --> 00:11:02,480
45% thought it would make shortages more common.

202
00:11:02,480 --> 00:11:05,480
Both of them were wrong.

203
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:06,480
A lot of them were wrong.

204
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:07,480
But the Russians were better.

205
00:11:07,480 --> 00:11:08,980
The Russians were better, closer

206
00:11:08,980 --> 00:11:10,480
to the truth than the Americans.

207
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:14,480
Here, living in the financial capital of the U.S.A.,

208
00:11:14,480 --> 00:11:18,480
most people think that speculating in grain creates problems.

209
00:11:18,480 --> 00:11:19,480
But wait a minute.

210
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:21,480
What is speculation in grain?

211
00:11:21,480 --> 00:11:23,980
That means holding it off

212
00:11:23,980 --> 00:11:26,480
for expecting higher prices later, right?

213
00:11:26,480 --> 00:11:28,980
Isn't that what you have to do

214
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:31,480
if you are managing a grain market?

215
00:11:31,480 --> 00:11:32,480
Now, think of it this way.

216
00:11:32,480 --> 00:11:35,480
And I'm coming now to agricultural future.

217
00:11:35,480 --> 00:11:38,980
In the most world, there is

218
00:11:38,980 --> 00:11:42,480
one harvest of wheat every year, okay?

219
00:11:42,480 --> 00:11:45,480
And that harvest comes in a certain time

220
00:11:45,480 --> 00:11:48,480
of the year, every year, once a year.

221
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:50,480
And it has to be held

222
00:11:50,480 --> 00:11:52,480
in storage over the year, right?

223
00:11:52,480 --> 00:11:54,480
Because people don't eat it all at once.

224
00:11:54,480 --> 00:11:56,480
You eat it over the whole year.

225
00:11:56,480 --> 00:11:58,480
So, somebody is storing grain.

226
00:11:58,480 --> 00:12:00,480
This is a fundamental problem, okay?

227
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:02,480
That's a business.

228
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:05,980
And you have to know that somewhere, there is some warehouse

229
00:12:05,980 --> 00:12:09,480
holding the grain that you will be consuming in six months' time.

230
00:12:09,480 --> 00:12:11,980
And that warehouse is run

231
00:12:11,980 --> 00:12:14,480
by some professionals who do that.

232
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:17,480
And if they think prices are higher later,

233
00:12:17,480 --> 00:12:20,480
they will keep it longer in the warehouse.

234
00:12:20,480 --> 00:12:22,480
And what does that do?

235
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:24,480
That evens out the price.

236
00:12:24,480 --> 00:12:26,480
It doesn't make it worse.

237
00:12:26,480 --> 00:12:28,980
If they think there is going to be a shortage of grain,

238
00:12:28,980 --> 00:12:31,480
they hold it back now and the price of grain goes up.

239
00:12:31,480 --> 00:12:32,980
And so, everyone starts consuming a

240
00:12:32,980 --> 00:12:34,480
little less because of the higher price.

241
00:12:34,480 --> 00:12:37,480
And it smooths things out and it works better.

242
00:12:37,480 --> 00:12:40,480
This is elementary economics, but it's

243
00:12:40,480 --> 00:12:43,480
not understood, I think, by most people.

244
00:12:43,480 --> 00:12:48,480
And the futures markets are just

245
00:12:48,480 --> 00:12:53,480
sophisticated markets that help that process.

246
00:12:53,480 --> 00:12:55,480
So, I'm going to start with

247
00:12:55,480 --> 00:12:57,480
agricultural futures in talking about this.

248
00:12:57,480 --> 00:12:58,480
And I want to start

249
00:12:58,480 --> 00:12:59,480
also with a very important point.

250
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:01,480
I'm going to start with agriculture.

251
00:13:01,480 --> 00:13:03,980
And I want to start with a very

252
00:13:03,980 --> 00:13:06,480
elementary example because it's the first futures market.

253
00:13:06,480 --> 00:13:08,480
So, where do you think futures markets started?

254
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:11,480
Anyone have a – you would think

255
00:13:11,480 --> 00:13:14,480
it started in New York or Chicago, right?

256
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:16,480
Or London, right?

257
00:13:16,480 --> 00:13:18,480
Or Paris.

258
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:23,980
It actually started in Japan in a place

259
00:13:23,980 --> 00:13:29,480
called Dojima, which is in the city of Osaka.

260
00:13:29,480 --> 00:13:31,480
And they started in the –

261
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:35,480
I think it was the early 1600s.

262
00:13:35,480 --> 00:13:42,980
And so, let's just go back, if we can

263
00:13:42,980 --> 00:13:50,480
go back to the year 1673 in Osaka, Japan.

264
00:13:50,480 --> 00:13:54,480
Japan was heavily dependent on rice, okay?

265
00:13:54,480 --> 00:13:57,480
And the rice farmers would farm all over

266
00:13:57,480 --> 00:14:00,480
Japan, but there was a rice market in Dojima,

267
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:02,480
which was the national rice market.

268
00:14:02,480 --> 00:14:06,980
And I have data here, according to a study,

269
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:11,480
there were 91 rice warehouses in Dojima in 1673.

